{
  "$schema": "../../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",

  /* ===========================================================================================
     THE MARKETING SITE, AND SINCE THE ONE-DOMAIN FLIP THE ADDRESS THE APP IS REACHED THROUGH.

     ONE DOMAIN. This Worker holds `brain.dog` as its Custom Domain and
     answers everything on it: the site's own pages from its assets, and the app's doors (`/app`,
     `/api/*`, `/auth/*`, `/account/*`, `/mcp`, `/in/*` and OAuth's `/oauth/*`, with the build id,
     OAuth's metadata under `/.well-known/` and `/healthz` beside them) handed to the app Worker
     through the `APP` binding. `run_worker_first` below is the exact list. The app itself lives at
     the root of `app.brain.dog` (`apps/worker/wrangler.jsonc`, production's routes); `/app` here is
     handed over only so the app Worker can answer an old `brain.dog/app/<rest>` page load with one
     308 to `app.brain.dog/<rest>`, and its API doors keep answering here for tokens handed out on
     this name.

     IT IS STILL A DEPLOYMENT OF ITS OWN. `apps/web` ships inside the app Worker's version as
     `assets` (33 §7); this does not, because it holds no record and a copy change here must never
     be the reason the record is down. Its pages are the prerendered routes under `src/routes`,
     built with `strict: true` so a route that quietly stopped prerendering fails the build rather
     than shipping blank (`apps/site/svelte.config.js`).

     A SCRIPT THAT RUNS FOR THE APP'S DOORS AND FOR NOTHING ELSE. This paragraph said the site had
     no `main` because a script here would never execute on the happy path "and executes on every
     404", a place for logic to accumulate. The second half is not what happens with
     `not_found_handling` "404-page": a miss is answered by the asset layer and never reaches the
     script. Measured through `wrangler dev -c site -c app` on 23 September:
     `/nope/deep/er` with and without an
     `Accept`, a POST and a PUT to it and a POST to `/pricing` answered the 404 page or a 405, and
     the script's log line appeared for none of them; it appeared for every path in
     `run_worker_first` and for no other. So `src/worker.ts` is one line that hands those paths to
     the app through the `APP` service binding, and the rule that the site holds no logic of its own
     is now a test (`test/forward.test.ts`) instead of an absent key. `main` is at the top level now
     that both environments are bound, and it was in `env.alpha` alone for one push because a
     top-level `main` is inherited: alpha rehearsed the token, the binding and a version rollback
     while production still uploaded its assets-only `no-op-worker.js`.
     The uptime watchdog used to be its cron; it is `apps/watchdog` now, a Worker that does not share this one's fate.

     Cloudflare Pages was the other candidate and is the wrong one:
     it is a second deployment system with its own build settings, its own preview semantics and
     its own dashboard, for a directory that `wrangler deploy` already knows how to upload. One
     tool, two configs, and `wrangler rollback` works the same way on both.

     THE SITE AND THE APP ROLL BACK INDEPENDENTLY, and that is a feature rather than an oversight:
     a marketing copy change cannot be the reason the record is unavailable, and a Durable Object
     migration cannot be the reason a price is wrong. The binding keeps that: it calls whichever
     version of the app is live, so an app deploy or rollback reaches the site's name with no site
     deploy, and a site rollback never moves the app.
     =========================================================================================== */

  "name": "braindog-site",

  /* Inherited by both environments. `services` is not inheritable, so each environment names its
     own app below, and the top level, which `deploy.yml` never deploys, binds
     none. */
  "main": "src/worker.ts",

  /* Required with or without code, and pinned to the Worker's own date for one reason: two numbers
     that mean "the platform behaviour we deployed against" should not be two numbers. */
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",

  /* The site's whole job is to arrive at one address. A `workers.dev` copy is a second URL for the
     same pages, indexable, with no canonical tag: a self-inflicted duplicate-content problem on the
     one page whose job is to be found. */
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,

  "observability": { "enabled": true },

  /* `directory` is relative to this file, so it is the build output beside it, unprefixed.

     `not_found_handling` IS "404-page": a path with no file answers a 404 status with `404.html`,
     which `src/routes/404` prerenders in the site's own shell. It was absent, which is "none", and
     a mistyped address got a blank white page. `"single-page-application"` would be wrong twice
     over: there is no client router here by default, and answering `index.html` with a 200 for every
     typo makes a site look alive to a crawler and dead to a reader. `test/content.test.ts` holds the
     setting and the file to each other. ONE PAGE AT EVERY DEPTH is why the build names its assets from the
     root (`svelte.config.js` `paths.relative: false`): `./_app/` from `/a/b` asks under `/a/`.

     `run_worker_first` IS THE APP'S BUILT DOORS, for the reasons at `env.alpha`, whose own
     `assets` replaces this block and so repeats the list. `apps/worker/test/site-binding.test.ts`
     fails if the two lists differ. */
  "assets": {
    "directory": "build",
    "not_found_handling": "404-page",
    "run_worker_first": ["/app", "/app/*", "/api/*", "/auth/*", "/account/*", "/mcp", "/mcp/", "/in/*", "/oauth/*", "/.well-known/braindog-build", "/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource", "/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/*", "/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server", "/healthz"]
  },

  /* Two environments for symmetry with `apps/worker/wrangler.jsonc`. Only production is deployed
     today; `alpha` is kept for a future rehearsal (below). `routes` is an inheritable key, so, exactly
     as in the Worker's config, it is declared per environment and never at the top level, or
     `--env alpha` would claim the apex.

     `assets` IS ALSO INHERITABLE, and alpha overrides it: an `assets` key inside an environment
     replaces the top-level one. Alpha serves `build-alpha`, the same pages built with
     `BRAINDOG_ENV=alpha` so its accent is its own; production keeps `build`. */
  "env": {
    /* PRODUCTION IS THE ONE ENVIRONMENT DEPLOYED. It holds `brain.dog`, the one domain, and hands
       the app's routes to `braindog-production`. The app keeps no name of its own beyond
       `app.brain.dog`, which sends a browser to `brain.dog`.

       `env.alpha` BELOW IS KEPT ONLY FOR A FUTURE REHEARSAL, AND IT IS NOT DEPLOYED. Its app Worker,
       `braindog-alpha`, was deleted on 25 September, so an alpha site
       deployed now would hand every door to a service that does not exist. `deploy.yml` runs its
       alpha steps only when the repository variable `DEPLOY_ALPHA` is `true`, so that variable stays
       unset while the app Worker is gone. The block stays so that a rehearsal, the day one is wanted
       again, needs the app Worker recreated and nothing rewritten here.

       THE APEX, AND `www` IS NOT HERE. A Worker can hold several Custom Domains, but this one's code
       runs only for the app's doors, and widening it to move `www` to the apex would be writing a
       request handler for a redirect Cloudflare performs at the edge for free. The `www` to apex
       redirect is a single dashboard Redirect Rule. */
    "production": {
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "brain.dog", "custom_domain": true }],
      "services": [{ "binding": "APP", "service": "braindog-production" }],
      /* NO SCHEDULE: the uptime check moved to `apps/watchdog`, but a deploy keeps a cron it is not told to drop, so the
         old every-minute trigger kept firing into a Worker with no `scheduled()` and failed once a minute. */
      "triggers": { "crons": [] }
    },

    /* EACH ENVIRONMENT HANDS ITS APP'S DOORS TO ITS OWN APP. A service binding in place of
       zone routes means no route is written, so the deploy token needs no Zone permission, and
       everything the move adds lives in the site's version, so undoing it is `wrangler rollback`.

       `run_worker_first` IS THE LIST OF DOORS THE APP HAS BUILT, AND ONLY THOSE. A built door left
       off it answers the site's 404 page on the site's own name and nothing fails at deploy time;
       `/a`, `/s` and `/ws` are in the app's own list and unbuilt, so they stay the site's 404 page
       here. `/mcp/` is listed beside `/mcp` because `servesMcp` accepts the slash and an MCP client
       configured with it reached the site's 404 page. The bare `/auth`,
       `/api`, `/in` and `/account` are not listed, as the old zone routes did not list them. The Worker
       claims `/.well-known/braindog-build` and the OAuth `.well-known` paths by name, never
       `/.well-known/*`, because `security.txt` is the site's.

       OAUTH IS A DOOR OF THE APP: `/oauth/*` is where an MCP client
       registers, sends the person's browser to sign in and trades its code, and the metadata it
       discovers from `/mcp`'s 401 is the protected resource's (with its `/mcp` form under it, as
       RFC 9728 inserts a resource's path) and the authorization server's. `oauth.ts` in the app
       answers every path under them, a 404 in OAuth's words for one that names nothing.

       `/healthz` IS THE APP'S LIVENESS ANSWER (deb5b703), matched in its `src/index.ts` ahead of
       every predicate in `src/route.ts`, and it was off this list for a while: `alpha.brain.dog/healthz`
       was this site's 404 page, a monitor's red from the wrong Worker, while
       `apps/worker/test/site-binding.test.ts` stayed green because it read only six of those
       predicates. That test now reads every
       branch the app's `route()` takes on a path and holds this list to them in both directions.

       `APP` NAMES THE SAME ENVIRONMENT'S APP. A site bound to the other environment's app would
       serve production's record on alpha's name, and the same test fails on it. */
    "alpha": {
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "alpha.brain.dog", "custom_domain": true }],
      "triggers": { "crons": [] },
      "assets": {
        "directory": "build-alpha",
        "not_found_handling": "404-page",
        "run_worker_first": ["/app", "/app/*", "/api/*", "/auth/*", "/account/*", "/mcp", "/mcp/", "/in/*", "/oauth/*", "/.well-known/braindog-build", "/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource", "/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/*", "/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server", "/healthz"]
      },
      "services": [{ "binding": "APP", "service": "braindog-alpha" }]
    }
  }
}
